#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096374 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/id #uuid "dccb5bac-e528-5b42-9949-36b3870fdde1" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-4287" :question/problem "Consider the picture below. Given that $\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}$, find $\\lambda$ and $\\mu$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1106/Source/Images/bfa592bb1f4e5a292e1e94476ab6e5f1.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1106/Source/Images/49fbbe1f5dd4731054af33666b88cb64.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup ONM$ shown above, we have:\n\n- $ON=2 \\mid \\mathbf{n} \\mid$;\n- $\\overrightarrow{ON}$ and $\\mathbf{n}$ have the same direction\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{ON}=2\\mathbf{n}\n$$\n\n.\n\nSimilarly:\n\n- $NM=2 \\mid \\mathbf{k} \\mid$\n- $\\overrightarrow{NM}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$ have the same direction\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{NM}=2\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\n.\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= ON^(→) + NM^(→) \\\\\n&= 2n + 2k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{m}=\\lambda\\mathbf{n}+\\mu\\mathbf{k}=2\\mathbf{n}+2\\mathbf{k}\n$$\n\n.\n\nSince $\\mathbf{n} \\nparallel \\mathbf{k}$, the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{n}$ and $\\mathbf{k}$, we get:\n\n- $\\lambda=2$ and $\\mu=2$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096375 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "feae36e7-ae88-5f3a-957f-e65a92e399ad" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Vector That Satisfies a Ratio in Terms of Known Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "09d593cc-526e-5691-9a18-efff515eae59" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-4288" :question/problem "Consider the following diagram, where $\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}$, $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf{b}$, and $X$ divides $AB$ in the ratio $3:1$. Find $\\overrightarrow{OX}$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1106/Source/Images/8512f8ab1660315b7e5f0aa8beb302ff.png)" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, we see that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OX} = \\overrightarrow{OA}+\\overrightarrow{AX}\n$$\n\nWe're told that\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{OA} = \\mathbf{a}\n$$\n\nso we just need to work out $\\overrightarrow{AX}$.\n\nFirst, from $\\triangle OAB$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB^(→) &= AO^(→) + OB^(→) \\\\\n&=-OA^(→) + OB^(→) \\\\\n&=-a + b \\\\\n&= b - a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $AX:XB=3:1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AX}=\\dfrac{3}{4}\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\dfrac{3}{4}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a})\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nOX^(→) &= OA^(→) + AX^(→) \\\\\n&= a + \\frac{3}{4}(b - a) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}a + \\frac{3}{4}b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096418 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2fbb8f19-f35a-59c4-9b77-8f6c3542ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor in a Linear Combination"}] :question/id #uuid "839ed5f5-165f-5de5-aba1-551ac605475f" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-5212" :question/problem "Consider the picture below. Given that $\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda\\mathbf{b}$, find $\\lambda$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1106/Source/Images/c2827bf97f6d17572e9ac2b808d16a69.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1106/Source/Images/0b673cb49bd6ea0955e57952763b7458.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup OAC$, we have:\n\n- $AC=2 \\mid \\mathbf{b} \\mid$\n- $\\overrightarrow{AC}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ have the same direction\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AC}=2\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nc &= OA^(→) + AC^(→) \\\\\n&= a + 2b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}=\\mathbf{a}+\\lambda\\mathbf{b}=\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b}\n$$\n\nSince $\\mathbf{a} \\nparallel \\mathbf{b}$, the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{b}$, we get:\n$\\lambda=2$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097082 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "45b6a076-8795-5510-aabc-62a925826dd0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Scalar Factors in a Linear Combination"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80120cea-42f6-536a-a12c-4c3b760859bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ = 1.5$ and $μ = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094899} {:answer/id #uuid "90ed0152-bc17-5c0a-9fac-769c5a688387" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ = 2$ and $μ = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094900} {:answer/id #uuid "b64b31a2-ea40-5bfb-917e-faf58d123194" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ =-2$ and $μ =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094901} {:answer/id #uuid "fe5f92d9-4714-55d4-8102-d106e968b4f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ =-2$ and $μ = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094902} {:answer/id #uuid "339e78f1-3955-5364-a7de-0d57fddd6b7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ = 2.5$ and $μ =-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90ed0152-bc17-5c0a-9fac-769c5a688387" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$λ = 2$ and $μ = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094900} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9f338b7-0e6c-5006-af37-19877673bf78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186094898}] :question/id #uuid "1b7aee39-22ab-5444-970d-797a67e85565" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-19370" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1106/Source/Images/q-19370.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c = λa + μb$, find $λ$ and $μ$."}]}
